1
00:00:07,410 --> 00:00:13,710
For someone who's potentially not a storyteller, if I asked you right now and I said, tell me five stories about you, go.

2
00:00:14,669 --> 00:00:19,170
You'd probably freak out and you'd probably say to me, ah, Vin, I don't have any stories to tell you.

3
00:00:20,070 --> 00:00:21,870
That's not a truthful answer.

4
00:00:22,250 --> 00:00:29,730
The truthful answer is actually, ah, Vin, I don't document my stories and as a result, I'm relying on my memory to remember it.

5
00:00:29,730 --> 00:00:32,229
And if I'm relying on my memory right now, I don't remember any of them.

6
00:00:32,890 --> 00:00:34,270
That's the real answer.

7
00:00:34,270 --> 00:00:40,329
Most people don't develop the habit of documenting stories and then as a result, they don't storytell.

8
00:00:40,570 --> 00:00:45,109
This is why as you learn the library of stories or how to build a library of stories,

9
00:00:45,289 --> 00:00:51,609
this is when you'll start to understand that documentation is a huge part of becoming a great storyteller.

10
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:57,549
So let's once and for all learn this great habit so that we can become great storytellers once and for all.

11
00:00:57,689 --> 00:01:02,689
The first step when it comes to building a library of stories is called...

12
00:01:04,409 --> 00:01:05,530
Eye of the Writer.

13
00:01:06,530 --> 00:01:09,810
Now, in life, I don't know if you suffer from this, but I do.

14
00:01:09,950 --> 00:01:15,409
In life, sometimes one day blends into another, one week blends into the other, one month blends into another,

15
00:01:15,590 --> 00:01:19,510
and then one year kind of blends into the other, and then all of a sudden, everything's just a blur.

16
00:01:20,150 --> 00:01:21,209
That happens to me.

17
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:24,709
Sometimes I get stuck in a mode where I'm living life on autopilot.

18
00:01:25,209 --> 00:01:32,530
And when I'm asking you to engage in Eye of the Writer, what I'm actually asking you to do is become more emotionally aware

19
00:01:33,209 --> 00:01:34,250
of what moves you.

20
00:01:34,269 --> 00:01:40,750
In life, become more emotionally aware of what moves you in life.

21
00:01:41,420 --> 00:01:47,989
Now, when you engage in Eye of the Writer and you become more emotionally aware, when something moves you, document it.

22
00:01:50,269 --> 00:01:51,209
Document it.

23
00:01:51,620 --> 00:01:59,849
And when you document things from this point in your life, moving forward into the future, that allows you to document stories moving forward.

24
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:04,230
So you capture the stories into the future, represented by the F.

25
00:02:04,590 --> 00:02:06,010
Now, of course, there's a lot of things that you can do.

26
00:02:06,030 --> 00:02:21,009
Of course, if you just make the commitment of capturing one story per week, if you make that your goal, then by the end of this year, you will have 20, 30, 40, 50 stories, if you just commit to capturing one story a week.

27
00:02:21,189 --> 00:02:26,449
And when something emotionally moves you, when you capture it, what you do is you do something like this.

28
00:02:27,449 --> 00:02:30,370
What you currently see is one of my templates.

29
00:02:30,689 --> 00:02:35,669
I use this template when I'm documenting stories, and you can use any note-taking app you want.

30
00:02:36,030 --> 00:02:39,990
But for me, the moment something emotionally moves me, I then fill in the blanks.

31
00:02:40,150 --> 00:02:44,650
Notice how I've got the incident there, and under the incident, I've got the who, what, where, and when.

32
00:02:44,849 --> 00:02:47,229
Vax, visual, auditory, kinesthetic, and smell.

33
00:02:47,550 --> 00:02:48,870
Jot down a few quick notes.

34
00:02:49,110 --> 00:02:53,849
And then I even write down the dialogue, key things that were said that really emotionally moved me.

35
00:02:54,210 --> 00:02:56,569
And then right on the bottom, I even write down a few points.

36
00:02:56,729 --> 00:02:58,169
What could this story be about?

37
00:02:58,870 --> 00:03:01,789
It takes me five to ten minutes to document this story.

38
00:03:02,530 --> 00:03:06,009
But the moment something moves me, I document it.

39
00:03:06,030 --> 00:03:06,889
Like you just saw.

40
00:03:07,090 --> 00:03:11,330
And again, once I've done that, I'm capturing stories moving forward into the future.

41
00:03:12,189 --> 00:03:14,169
Now, what about the past, though?

42
00:03:14,710 --> 00:03:17,330
Because there are a bunch of stories in your past.

43
00:03:17,810 --> 00:03:21,129
You've been through thousands of experiences in your life.

44
00:03:21,389 --> 00:03:25,969
Some of them exciting, some of them thrilling, some of them sad, some of them maybe boring.

45
00:03:26,210 --> 00:03:32,509
But within these experiences that make up you as a person, embedded in those experiences are stories.

46
00:03:33,169 --> 00:03:36,009
Now, we need to be able to look to the past for the story.

47
00:03:36,009 --> 00:03:42,949
Now, one of the ways to look to the past, I call this the FACTS ROLL STRATEGY.

48
00:03:42,969 --> 00:03:44,669
The FACTS ROLL STRATEGY.

49
00:03:44,669 --> 00:03:47,689
And that allows you to capture stories into the past.

50
00:03:48,129 --> 00:03:51,009
This way, you capture stories as a habit moving forward into the future.

51
00:03:51,110 --> 00:03:55,990
But you can work on one exercise that can help you harvest stories from the past.

52
00:03:56,509 --> 00:03:59,650
Now, I know the FACTS ROLL STRATEGY isn't the most eloquent name.

53
00:03:59,789 --> 00:04:05,830
However, the ingredients you need to execute this strategy to harvest the stories from the past is you need a FACTS ROLL.

54
00:04:06,009 --> 00:04:07,830
And a FACTS ROLL is just a roll of paper.

55
00:04:07,990 --> 00:04:12,889
And a FACTS MACHINE is just a machine we used to use in the ancient past when we're communicating.

56
00:04:13,389 --> 00:04:17,670
Don't worry about what it is, but just get a FACTS ROLL OF PAPER or a ROLL OF PAPER.

57
00:04:17,889 --> 00:04:21,750
Then you also need lots of colored crayons to bring out the inner child.

58
00:04:22,129 --> 00:04:24,750
And then you also need some people that have known you for a while.

59
00:04:24,930 --> 00:04:31,689
So whether it's friends or family, try to grab two to three people that have known you the longest, because that will make the strategy the most effective.

60
00:04:32,769 --> 00:04:35,990
Once you have these ingredients, take the ROLL OF PAPER.

61
00:04:36,009 --> 00:04:39,810
And then you roll it out, you roll it all the way across the room.

62
00:04:40,629 --> 00:04:46,629
Now on one side, on this side of the FACTS ROLL or on this side of the ROLL OF PAPER, write down the day you were born.

63
00:04:46,769 --> 00:04:47,649
So birth date.

64
00:04:48,069 --> 00:04:54,009
Draw a really long line all the way across to over here and draw another line and then write present day.

65
00:04:54,490 --> 00:04:59,529
And then in between, start making a few markings. Over here, write earliest memory.

66
00:05:00,730 --> 00:05:04,009
First time you had a crush in junior primary school or in primary school.

67
00:05:04,170 --> 00:05:05,509
Then when you went to high school,

68
00:05:05,509 --> 00:05:14,750
maybe a first job, first time you had a kiss, first time you got into a job and then you got fired, you know, first time you got detention.

69
00:05:15,149 --> 00:05:22,529
So mark down those key areas, first real professional job, university, and just mark down all the key errors of your life.

70
00:05:22,689 --> 00:05:26,069
And if you need help during this section, check out this here on screen.

71
00:05:26,350 --> 00:05:31,110
As you look at this, these are the things that will help you jolt some ideas.

72
00:05:32,290 --> 00:05:35,490
And I'll give you a moment to take a screenshot of this, because I think this is very useful.

73
00:05:35,509 --> 00:05:42,209
I find it useful, you know, think of places, even put on some music that reminds you of that era of life.

74
00:05:42,389 --> 00:05:44,310
It usually brings back a lot of memories.

75
00:05:45,410 --> 00:05:50,089
Now, once you've done this and you've marked it all out, give all of your friends a crayon.

76
00:05:50,110 --> 00:05:52,889
And if you're old enough, maybe have a little bit of wine.

77
00:05:53,050 --> 00:05:54,470
It might help with the experience.

78
00:05:54,850 --> 00:06:01,449
And then say to your friends, hey, do you remember any stories or any experiences that we've had together that you remember of me?

79
00:06:02,029 --> 00:06:04,569
And then you're going to have friends say to you, oh, do you remember that time?

80
00:06:04,569 --> 00:06:09,209
You really liked Stacy, and you did that joke in class, and then no one laughed, and then you felt terrible about yourself,

81
00:06:09,290 --> 00:06:11,430
and then you ended up trying to move schools because you were so awkward?

82
00:06:13,149 --> 00:06:14,730
You'll have friends, do you remember that time at camp?

83
00:06:15,069 --> 00:06:18,850
Do you remember that time you got that first job, and then you got fired because you tried to steal money?

84
00:06:19,110 --> 00:06:24,250
Or your friends will remind you of stories left, right, and center, and they will jolt your memory.

85
00:06:24,889 --> 00:06:28,410
You know that story of me when I changed my name from Quang to Vinh?

86
00:06:28,930 --> 00:06:30,430
I'd forgotten that story.

87
00:06:30,649 --> 00:06:33,589
I believe my brain psychologically blocked it out.

88
00:06:33,589 --> 00:06:37,930
And when I did this process, my best mate Lenny reminded me of this story.

89
00:06:38,189 --> 00:06:41,230
He said, dude, do you remember that time you changed your name from Quang to Vinh?

90
00:06:41,410 --> 00:06:44,550
I called you Quang for like 12 years, and then you made me call you Vinh?

91
00:06:44,709 --> 00:06:45,850
It was so weird.

92
00:06:46,569 --> 00:06:48,209
I'd forgotten that story.

93
00:06:48,629 --> 00:06:54,329
Yet that's one of the most powerful stories I use when I speak to the youth about transformation,

94
00:06:54,949 --> 00:06:58,009
creating a new version of yourself, about breaking the mold.

95
00:06:58,410 --> 00:06:59,930
I use that story.

96
00:07:00,629 --> 00:07:03,569
You will be shocked at how many stories you can tell.

97
00:07:03,589 --> 00:07:06,930
And I have my students send me photos all the time.

98
00:07:07,069 --> 00:07:08,250
The most recent one was this photo.

99
00:07:08,370 --> 00:07:08,810
Check it out.

100
00:07:09,350 --> 00:07:12,029
In this photo, you see my students doing the process.

101
00:07:12,310 --> 00:07:15,470
And in that one session, that student of mine said to me,

102
00:07:15,529 --> 00:07:18,930
she came up with over 30 different stories in one sitting.

103
00:07:19,949 --> 00:07:21,430
Go through this process.

104
00:07:21,689 --> 00:07:24,730
This is one of the most therapeutic things you can do with your friends.

105
00:07:24,870 --> 00:07:26,389
You will love this experience.

106
00:07:27,670 --> 00:07:32,389
Now that you've done this, you'll find you have a back pocket full of stories that you can use now.

107
00:07:32,389 --> 00:07:38,189
Never again will you fall victim to small talk that might be uncomfortable and awkward.

108
00:07:38,410 --> 00:07:40,490
Now you've got great stories to be able to share.

109
00:07:40,670 --> 00:07:44,550
And one of the most beautiful things about storytelling is the moment you share a story,

110
00:07:44,670 --> 00:07:47,629
it encourages other people to share stories too.

111
00:07:47,850 --> 00:07:50,009
And then again, that's when real connection happens.

112
00:07:50,509 --> 00:07:51,149
All right.

113
00:07:51,189 --> 00:07:52,389
So that's the first step.

114
00:07:52,529 --> 00:07:53,269
Eye of the writer.

115
00:07:53,509 --> 00:07:54,430
Engage in it more.

116
00:07:54,750 --> 00:07:56,790
Document it when something emotionally moves you.

117
00:07:56,970 --> 00:07:58,350
Capture stories into the future.

118
00:07:58,550 --> 00:08:02,170
Use the Facts World strategy to capture stories in the past.

119
00:08:02,389 --> 00:08:07,870
The second step is called eye of the V slash P.

120
00:08:08,050 --> 00:08:09,990
Eye of the V slash P.

121
00:08:10,189 --> 00:08:14,649
And what I mean by V slash P is videographer and photographer.

122
00:08:15,029 --> 00:08:17,750
Eye of the videographer and eye of the photographer.

123
00:08:18,930 --> 00:08:24,689
When I started working with more videographers, they had this great line and Craig always used to say this to me.

124
00:08:24,810 --> 00:08:28,970
He'd say to me, what you can show, don't tell.

125
00:08:29,449 --> 00:08:31,870
What you can show, don't tell.

126
00:08:33,990 --> 00:08:40,070
We live in the modern world now, and we no longer have to rely just on our voice to tell stories.

127
00:08:40,269 --> 00:08:44,710
We can bring the characters to life by showing a photo and also showing a video.

128
00:08:45,009 --> 00:08:51,210
So that means a part of this documentation process is when you're engaging an eye of the writer and something moves you emotionally,

129
00:08:51,509 --> 00:08:52,909
of course, document it.

130
00:08:52,990 --> 00:08:55,509
But if you can, take a photo or a video.

131
00:08:56,350 --> 00:08:57,649
Let me ask you this question.

132
00:08:57,909 --> 00:09:02,269
During the storytelling module, I shared a story with that accountant.

133
00:09:02,429 --> 00:09:03,090
I showed us.

134
00:09:03,090 --> 00:09:04,470
I shared a story about the accountant.

135
00:09:05,210 --> 00:09:07,230
How connected did you feel to that accountant?

136
00:09:07,789 --> 00:09:10,409
You probably didn't feel that connected to him.

137
00:09:10,610 --> 00:09:12,669
That's because I didn't even show you a photo of him.

138
00:09:12,750 --> 00:09:13,870
I didn't show you a video.

139
00:09:14,250 --> 00:09:19,110
Now, I have photos and I have videos, but I didn't show you because I wanted to illustrate this example.

140
00:09:19,450 --> 00:09:21,490
You probably didn't feel that connected to the accountant.

141
00:09:22,370 --> 00:09:25,450
However, how much more connected did you feel to my grandmother?

142
00:09:25,769 --> 00:09:27,210
Did you feel more connected to her?

143
00:09:27,929 --> 00:09:31,809
You probably did because I showed you videos and photos of my grandmother.

144
00:09:31,809 --> 00:09:37,929
I was able to successfully bring her to life for you so you were better able to connect with her in that story.

145
00:09:38,210 --> 00:09:41,429
Can you see the importance of eye of the videographer and eye of the photographer?

146
00:09:41,809 --> 00:09:46,929
So when you can, when you're documenting the story, like you can see in this example here.

147
00:09:47,090 --> 00:09:54,190
In this example, when I was documenting this particular story, if you look over there, I've also documented the photos and the videos.

148
00:09:54,629 --> 00:10:01,789
Now, depending on what note-taking app you use, a lot of them are going to allow you to store text and images and videos.

149
00:10:01,789 --> 00:10:03,789
All in the same notes file.

150
00:10:04,909 --> 00:10:10,850
This means that anytime you want to access this story, it has the incident all there, all the ingredients.

151
00:10:11,029 --> 00:10:17,029
It's got the points and it also has the photos and videos all in one place for you to be able to access.

152
00:10:17,330 --> 00:10:24,620
Which now brings us to the third step in the formula, which is eye of the director.

153
00:10:24,960 --> 00:10:25,960
Eye of the director.

154
00:10:26,360 --> 00:10:31,360
And with eye of the director, that's when you're sitting down with the lens of a director and you're going, hmm.

155
00:10:32,039 --> 00:10:33,500
What could this story be?

156
00:10:33,519 --> 00:10:34,620
What could this story symbolize?

157
00:10:34,879 --> 00:10:35,899
Does this sound familiar?

158
00:10:36,340 --> 00:10:42,299
Remember, when we're storytelling, we write down the story and then we write down all the words that the story could symbolize.

159
00:10:42,679 --> 00:10:44,720
This is what you do with eye of the director.

160
00:10:44,960 --> 00:10:50,700
So you sit down with your particular story that you've documented with the incident, the point, the pictures and the videos.

161
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,759
And then you start to write down some of the words that this story could symbolize.

162
00:10:54,919 --> 00:10:57,940
That is you engaging in eye of the director.

163
00:10:58,500 --> 00:11:03,259
Because a director, they always look at stories and they go, which angle can I tell this story from?

164
00:11:03,519 --> 00:11:07,779
Like if it's a horror movie or a murder story, should I tell it from the murderer's point of view?

165
00:11:07,899 --> 00:11:09,840
Should I tell it from the neighbor's point of view?

166
00:11:09,960 --> 00:11:12,820
Should I tell it from the children's point of view and the family that got murdered?

167
00:11:12,980 --> 00:11:14,820
Or should I tell it from the parent's point of view?

168
00:11:14,940 --> 00:11:16,639
Sorry to use such a dark example.

169
00:11:16,940 --> 00:11:18,159
But that's what a director does.

170
00:11:18,500 --> 00:11:22,419
And that's what you do the very next time someone wants you to do a presentation.

171
00:11:22,779 --> 00:11:25,240
You sit down and you engage with eye of the director.

172
00:11:25,600 --> 00:11:28,980
And you write down all the words that the story could symbolize.

173
00:11:29,139 --> 00:11:32,960
And there you now have all the ingredients.

174
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:38,740
And all the preparation is now done for you to become a better storyteller.

175
00:11:38,860 --> 00:11:42,799
People often don't storytell because they don't document it.

176
00:11:42,899 --> 00:11:45,179
Now you have the documentation process.

177
00:11:45,460 --> 00:11:49,230
I hope this helps you on your journey of becoming a masterful storyteller.

